The hypothesis that $\pi_1(\unsure{f'|f})\unsure{=|\simeq}\pi_1(g)$ therefore means that there exists $\alpha\in\pi_1(Y)$, such that
\[
\pi_1(j')(\sigma)=\inserted{\operatorname{int}(\alpha)}\pi_1(j)(\sigma)
\]
for every $\sigma\in\operatorname{Im}(\pi_1(X)\xrightarrow{\pi_1(f)}\pi_1(U))$. Now this image is an \uline{open} subgroup of $\pi_1(U)$ (always true for a dominant morphism!). \deleted{Besides} Thus we are reduced to this: Let $U$ be an open subset \inserted{$\ne\varnothing$} of $Y$, and $a\in G$, such that $\tau_aU\subset Y$ [and such that (denoting by $j,j'$ the \deleted{maps} \inserted{morphisms} $y\longrightarrow y$ and $y\longmapsto y+a$ from $U$ to $Y$) $\pi_1(j)$ and $\pi_1(j')$ coincide as outer homomorphisms on an open subgroup of $\pi_1(U)$] -- \uline{then} $j=j'$ that is $a=0$.

\margin{\uline{Note well} this is no stronger than the corollary, as one sees by taking for $X$ the finite étale covering of $U$ corresponding to an open subgroup of $\pi_1(U)$.}

Finally, I realize that I cannot manage to prove it by geometric means -- for this I fall back on an \uline{arithmetic} method.
\margin{Yes -- see below!}

\deleted{\unsure{I will have} moreover \deleted{\unsure{weak}} the geometric theorem \unsure{generalized}; one shows that (at the cost of replacing $X$ by a nonempty connected open subset, which neither destroys the hypotheses nor modifies the conclusion) we may assume that $X$ is a variety}
